window.TOPICS = [
{k:"method",n:"Exam method",s:"How to answer Sir Barry's problems",b:[
{k:"box",tone:"blue",title:"The exam (from your notes)",x:["Saturday, <b>7 November 2026, 2:00–4:00 p.m.</b> Coverage: <b>Arts. 1–11</b> (through justifying circumstances). Sir will give details on Tuesday (3 Nov). Update this page after class.","Block intel: <b>written, blue-book, 7–8 bar-type questions</b>; he <b>reuses</b> questions; <b>IRAC</b> is expected. Fact patterns mix and switch the facts of the syllabus cases.","Budget: 120 minutes ÷ 8 questions ≈ <b>14 minutes each</b>, about 3 minutes to spot issues and 10 to write. Answer the ones you're sure of first."]},
{k:"h",x:"The answer skeleton (IRAC, written CLEAR-style)"},
{k:"tbl",head:["Step","What to write","Example (Ah Chong-type)"],rows:[
["<b>C</b>onclusion","Answer the call of the question in the first line.","No, A is not criminally liable."],
["<b>L</b>egal basis","Quote or paraphrase the codal, then Reyes's elements.","Under Art. 3, a felony by dolo requires freedom, intelligence and intent; mistake of fact negates intent if (1)…(3)…"],
["<b>E</b>xception","The trap or exception that could flip the answer.","Unless the mistake was due to his own fault or negligence (Oanis)."],
["<b>A</b>nalysis","Apply each element to a specific fact. Name the case whose facts match.","Here, as in Ah Chong, it was dark, the intruder forced the door…"],
["<b>R</b>estatement","Close with the conclusion and its consequence (civil liability, crime, stage).","Hence A is not liable; no civil liability either, since the act would have been justified."]]},
{k:"box",tone:"yellow",title:"Combination checklist: run every fact pattern through these, in order",x:[
"<b>1. Jurisdiction (Art. 2):</b> where? On what vessel (registry, not owner's nationality)? Is it in transit? A public officer abroad?",
"<b>2. Is there a felony (Art. 3)?</b> Act or omission + punishable + dolo/culpa. Mistake of fact? Mala prohibita (special law → intent is irrelevant)?",
"<b>3. Result different from intended (Art. 4(1))?</b> Error in personae, aberratio ictus, praeter intentionem → proximate cause → efficient intervening cause?",
"<b>4. Impossible crime (Art. 4(2))?</b> Against persons or property? Inherent impossibility or inadequate/ineffectual means? No other crime committed?",
"<b>5. Stage (Art. 6)?</b> Overt acts? Subjective phase complete? Desistance? Crimes with no frustrated stage (rape, theft)?",
"<b>6. Light felony (Art. 7)? Conspiracy/proposal (Art. 8)?</b> Is it punishable by itself, or only a mode of liability?",
"<b>7. Classification (Art. 9) and special law (Art. 10)</b> when the problem asks.",
"<b>8. Justifying circumstance (Art. 11)?</b> Unlawful aggression first; if it is missing, there is no self-defense (complete or incomplete).",
"<b>9. Civil liability:</b> none if justified, except Art. 11(4) (the persons benefited pay)."]},
{k:"box",tone:"green",title:"Facts matter to him",x:["He nitpicks case facts in recitation, and his problems are case facts with one detail switched. For every case, know which fact <b>decided</b> it (the dark room in Ah Chong; the sleeping victim in Oanis; the victim hiding in Sy Pio; the lighted chapel in Jaurigue). Drill <b>Facts → ruling</b> in the Cases tab.","Write the doctrine; cite the case only when you are sure of the name. Doctrine alone gets the points."]},
{k:"gatelist"}
]},

{k:"intro",n:"Introduction & Art. 1",s:"Readings, theories, characteristics",a:["1"],b:[
{k:"codal",a:"1"},
{k:"h",x:"Reyes: the theories behind the Code"},
{k:"p",x:"The RPC took effect on <b>1 January 1932</b>. It is based mainly on the <b>classical school</b>, with some <b>positivist</b> provisions (impossible crimes under Art. 4(2), juvenile delinquency, the indeterminate sentence)."},
{k:"tbl",head:["","Classical (basis of the RPC)","Positivist"],rows:[["Basis of liability","Human free will; the penalty is retribution","Crime is a social and natural phenomenon"],["Focus","The act and its result","The offender"],["Penalty","Fixed, in direct proportion to the crime","Individualized measures, after study by experts"],["In the RPC","Fixed penalties; mitigating and aggravating circumstances","Art. 4(2) impossible crime; minors"]]},
{k:"h",x:"Reyes: characteristics of criminal law"},
{k:"ul",x:["<b>Generality</b>: penal laws bind all who live or sojourn in the Philippines, citizens or not (Civ. Code Art. 14). Exceptions: <b>treaties</b> (VFA), <b>laws of preferential application</b> (RA 75), and <b>public international law</b> (heads of state, ambassadors, ministers plenipotentiary and resident, chargés d'affaires; <b>consuls are not exempt</b>).","<b>Territoriality</b>: enforced within Philippine territory (land, atmosphere, interior waters, maritime zone). Exceptions: Art. 2's five cases.","<b>Prospectivity</b>: a penal law cannot punish an act that was not punishable when committed. Exception: Art. 22, a law favorable to the accused is retroactive unless he is a <b>habitual criminal</b>.","<b>Pro reo / in dubio pro reo</b>: penal laws are construed strictly against the State and liberally in favor of the accused (presumption of innocence).","<b>Nullum crimen, nulla poena sine lege</b>: there is no crime without a law that punishes it. There are no common-law crimes in the Philippines."]},
{k:"box",tone:"blue",title:"Constitutional limits on penal laws",x:["No <b>ex post facto</b> law, no <b>bill of attainder</b> (Art. III § 22); <b>due process and equal protection</b> (§ 1); no <b>cruel, degrading or inhuman</b> punishment and no excessive fines (§ 19).","An ex post facto law makes criminal an act done before the law took effect, aggravates a crime, increases its penalty, or alters the rules of evidence to the accused's disadvantage, all applied retroactively."]},
{k:"h",x:"The introductory readings (short summary)"},
{k:"ul",x:["<b>Shavell, “Criminal Law”</b> (economic analysis): criminal law exists because fines alone cannot deter: offenders may lack wealth, may escape detection, or may gain too much from the crime. Imprisonment both deters and <b>incapacitates</b>.","<b>Cavanaugh, “The Justification of Punishment”</b>: <b>reductivism</b> (deterrence, incapacitation, reform: punish to prevent future crime) vs. <b>retributivism</b> (punish because it is deserved, in proportion to the crime); also <b>denunciation</b>. Maps onto the classical (retribution) vs. positivist (reform) schools.","<b>Restorative justice</b> (Gilman; Marshall): the parties with a stake in an offense resolve its aftermath together: victim-offender mediation, family group conferencing. Its focus is on repairing harm and reintegrating the offender. Philippine echoes: diversion under RA 9344, barangay conciliation.","<b>Rossabi, “The Colonial Roots of Criminal Procedure in the Philippines”</b>: Philippine law is a synthesis of indigenous, Spanish (Penal Code of 1887; church–state union) and American (General Order No. 58; common-law procedure) sources. The RPC itself replaced the Spanish Penal Code on 1 Jan. 1932."]},
{k:"exam",x:"“The RPC follows the classical theory: the basis of criminal liability is free will, and the penalty is retribution in proportion to the act. Positivist features exist, such as Art. 4(2), which punishes the offender's criminal tendency even though no felony is produced.”"},
{k:"drill",x:[["A law passed today increases the penalty for an act done last year. Can it apply?","No. It is an ex post facto law (it aggravates the penalty retroactively)."],["A new law lowers the penalty; the accused is a habitual criminal. Does he benefit?","No. Art. 22 excludes habitual criminals from retroactive favorable laws."],["Is a foreign consul who stabs someone in Makati immune?","No. Consuls are not covered by the public-international-law exception (only diplomatic agents such as ambassadors are)."]]}
]},

{k:"a2",n:"Art. 2 · Application",s:"Territoriality & extraterritoriality",a:["2"],b:[
{k:"codal",a:"2"},
{k:"h",x:"Reyes: scope"},
{k:"ul",x:["<b>Philippine archipelago</b>: the islands plus the <b>atmosphere</b>, <b>interior waters</b> (bays, rivers, lakes, straits) and <b>maritime zone</b>. Today: territorial sea 12 nautical miles; contiguous zone 24; EEZ 200.","<b>Philippine ship or airship</b>: one <b>registered</b> in the Philippines under Philippine law. The <b>nationality of the owner does not matter</b>; registry does. Para. 1 applies only when the vessel is <b>outside</b> our territory (on the high seas); in a foreign country's waters, that country's law governs, unless the crime affects only internal management (as a matter of comity).","<b>Para. 2–3</b> protect the country's credit and currency: forgery or counterfeiting of <b>Philippine</b> coins, notes and government securities, and introducing them, wherever done.","<b>Para. 4</b>: public officers or employees abroad, for offenses committed <b>in the exercise of their functions</b> (e.g., bribery, malversation, falsification by a consul). Crimes that have nothing to do with their functions (e.g., bigamy by an embassy clerk) are <b>not</b> covered.","<b>Para. 5</b>: crimes against national security and the law of nations (Title One, Book Two): treason, espionage, inciting to war, violation of neutrality, piracy and mutiny. <b>Rebellion is not included</b> (it is a crime against public order, Title Three)."]},
{k:"box",tone:"blue",title:"Foreign merchant vessels in our waters",x:["<b>French rule</b>: not triable unless the crime affects the peace and security of the territory or the safety of the state. <b>English rule</b> (ours): triable <b>unless</b> it affects only things within the vessel or its internal management.","<b>In transit</b> + mere possession of a prohibited drug → not triable (no breach of public order) (<i>Look Chaw</i>). Landing or using the drug here → triable. <b>Terminal port</b> (destination) → importation consummated (<i>Ah Sing</i>). Smoking opium aboard while anchored → breach of public order, triable (<i>Wong Cheng</i>). A continuing offense that continues into our waters → triable (<i>Bull</i>).","<b>Warships</b> are always treated as the territory of their country; offenses aboard a foreign warship are not triable here."]},
{k:"h",x:"Doctrines"},
{k:"cases",ids:["bull","vanwilsem","wongcheng","lookchaw","ahsing","lollo"]},
{k:"box",tone:"yellow",title:"Traps",x:["<b>Owner's nationality ≠ registry.</b> A Panamanian-registered ship owned by a Filipino is not a Philippine ship (2000 Bar).","<b>Bigamy abroad</b> (a second marriage in Singapore) is not covered by Art. 2, so it is not triable here (1994 Bar), even if the couple later lives here as husband and wife.","<b>A foreigner killing a foreigner on a foreign ship docked in Manila</b> → triable here (territoriality + generality; 2019/2015 Bar).","<b>Rebellion</b> abroad is not in Art. 2(5); <b>treason, espionage, piracy</b> are."]},
{k:"exam",x:"“Under the territoriality principle, the RPC applies to crimes committed within the Philippine archipelago, including its interior waters. A crime aboard a foreign merchant vessel within our waters is triable here, under the English rule we follow, unless it concerns only the ship's internal management (People v. Wong Cheng).”"},
{k:"drill",x:[["Bull, but the cattle were unloaded in Formosa before the ship entered our waters.","Not triable here: the offense no longer continued in Philippine waters."],["Look Chaw, but he only kept the opium in his cabin and never landed or sold it.","Not triable: mere possession in transit is not a breach of public order."],["Wong Cheng, but aboard a foreign warship.","Not triable: warships are the territory of their flag state."],["A Filipino consul in Hong Kong falsifies a passport in the course of his functions.","Triable here: Art. 2(4)."]]}
]},

{k:"a3",n:"Art. 3 · Felonies",s:"Dolo, culpa, mistake of fact",a:["3"],b:[
{k:"codal",a:"3"},
{k:"h",x:"Reyes: elements and kinds"},
{k:"ul",x:["<b>Felonies</b> are acts and omissions punishable by the <b>RPC</b>; “offense” is used for special laws, “misdemeanor” for minor infractions, and “crime” covers all.","<b>Act</b>: any bodily movement tending to produce an effect in the external world; it must be <b>external</b> (thoughts alone are not punished). <b>Omission</b>: failure to perform a positive duty required by law (e.g., failing to render assistance, Art. 275).","<b>Elements</b>: (1) an act or omission; (2) punishable by the RPC; (3) done by dolo or culpa. The act must be <b>voluntary</b>, meaning committed with <b>freedom and intelligence</b>.","<b>Dolo</b> requires <b>freedom, intelligence, intent</b>. Lacking any of them → no dolo. <b>Culpa</b> requires freedom, intelligence, and <b>imprudence (lack of skill) or negligence (lack of foresight)</b>. Imprudence = a deficiency of <b>action</b>; negligence = a deficiency of <b>perception</b>.","<b>Intent</b> is a mental state, presumed from the doing of an unlawful act, but the presumption is rebuttable. <b>General</b> criminal intent is presumed; <b>specific</b> intent (intent to kill, intent to gain) must be proven.","<b>Motive vs. intent</b>: intent is the purpose to use a particular means to achieve a result; motive is the reason that impels the act. Motive is not an element and is <b>immaterial when the accused is positively identified</b>; it matters when identity is in doubt or the evidence is circumstantial.","<b><i>Actus non facit reum, nisi mens sit rea</i></b>: the act does not make a person guilty unless the mind is guilty. <b><i>Actus me invito factus non est meus actus</i></b>: an act done by me against my will is not my act."]},
{k:"box",tone:"blue",title:"Mistake of fact (ignorantia facti excusat)",x:["Requisites (Reyes): (1) the act would have been <b>lawful</b> had the facts been as the accused believed them to be; (2) the accused's <b>intention</b> was lawful; (3) the mistake was <b>without fault or carelessness</b> on his part.","It is a defense only in <b>intentional</b> felonies (it negates intent). If the mistake came from negligence, he may still be liable for a culpable felony.","<b>Ignorance of the law excuses no one</b> (Civ. Code Art. 3); only mistakes of <b>fact</b> excuse."]},
{k:"tbl",head:["","Mala in se","Mala prohibita"],rows:[["Nature","Wrong in itself","Wrong only because prohibited"],["Law","Generally the RPC","Generally special laws"],["Intent / good faith","A defense","Not a defense; only the intent to do the prohibited act is needed"],["Stages, participation, circumstances","Considered","Generally not (unless the law adopts RPC nomenclature)"]]},
{k:"h",x:"Doctrines"},
{k:"cases",ids:["ahchong","oanis","magsumbol","gervero"]},
{k:"box",tone:"yellow",title:"Traps",x:["<b>Ah Chong vs. Oanis</b> turns on one fact: <b>was there time to verify?</b> Dark room, sudden attack → mistake excuses. Sleeping victim, ample time → it does not.","Mistake of fact vs. <b>error in personae</b>: in error in personae the act is a felony whoever the victim is (you meant to kill B, killed C) → liable. In mistake of fact, the act would have been <b>lawful</b> if the facts were as believed.","Shooting deliberately while mistaken is <b>dolo</b>, not reckless imprudence (Oanis: murder, not homicide through reckless imprudence).","In crimes under special laws (mala prohibita), good faith and lack of intent are generally <b>not</b> defenses."]},
{k:"exam",x:"“There is no felony by dolo without intent. Under the doctrine of mistake of fact, the accused is not liable if the act would have been lawful had the facts been as he believed, his intention was lawful, and the mistake was without fault or carelessness on his part (U.S. v. Ah Chong). If he had time to ascertain the facts and did not, the mistake does not excuse (People v. Oanis).”"},
{k:"drill",x:[["Ah Chong, but the lights were on and Pascual called out his name.","No mistake of fact: there was no reasonable basis for the belief → homicide."],["Oanis, but the man in bed suddenly reached for a gun under the pillow.","There is now reason to act at once; a mistake of fact or fulfillment of duty becomes arguable."],["Magsumbol, but the boundary was clearly fenced and marked.","Good faith is implausible → intent to gain may be inferred → theft."]]}
]},

{k:"a4a",n:"Art. 4(1) · Different result",s:"Proximate cause; error in personae, aberratio ictus, praeter intentionem",a:["4"],b:[
{k:"codal",a:"4"},
{k:"h",x:"Reyes: paragraph 1"},
{k:"p",x:"<b>One who commits an intentional felony is responsible for all the consequences that may naturally and logically result from it, whether foreseen or intended or not.</b> <i>El que es causa de la causa es causa del mal causado</i>: he who is the cause of the cause is the cause of the evil caused."},
{k:"ul",x:["<b>Requisites</b>: (1) an <b>intentional felony</b> has been committed; (2) the wrong done is the <b>direct, natural and logical consequence</b> of the felony.","“Committing a felony” means the act must be a <b>felony</b>: no felony, no liability for the result (e.g., a lawful act that accidentally injures). A <b>culpable</b> felony that causes a result is governed by Art. 365, not Art. 4(1).","<b>Creating an immediate sense of danger</b>: if the felonious act makes the victim, out of fear, do something that injures him (jumping into a river, running into traffic), the offender is liable (e.g., <i>U.S. v. Valdez</i>: the victim jumped into the river after a threat with a knife).","<b>Proximate cause</b>: the cause which, in natural and continuous sequence, unbroken by any efficient intervening cause, produces the injury, and without which the result would not have occurred. The felony need not be the immediate cause; it is enough that it set off the chain (<i>Iligan</i>).","Felony is <b>not</b> the proximate cause when (a) an active force intervened that is distinct and foreign to the felony, or (b) the result is due to the victim's own intentional act.","<b>Not</b> efficient intervening causes: the victim's weak or diseased condition (<i>Garcia</i>: heart disease); his nervousness or temperament; causes inherent in him (not knowing how to swim); neglect by the victim or a third person (refusing treatment); erroneous or unskillful medical treatment.","Death is <b>presumed</b> to be the natural consequence of the injuries if the victim was in <b>normal health</b>, death could be <b>expected</b> from the injuries, and it followed within a <b>reasonable time</b>.","Compare <i>Urbano v. IAC</i> (not on the syllabus): death from tetanus contracted 22 days after a hacking wound was due to an <b>efficient intervening cause</b> (the infection set in later), so there was no liability for the death."]},
{k:"tbl",head:["Situation","What happened","Effect on liability"],rows:[["<b>Error in personae</b>","Mistake in identity: the intended victim is not the one hit (both may be absent/present)","Liable for the crime committed; under Art. 49 the penalty is the lesser of the crime intended and the crime committed, in its maximum period (only if the crimes differ)"],["<b>Aberratio ictus</b>","Mistake in the blow: aimed at A, it hit B (both present)","Usually a complex crime under Art. 48 (e.g., attempted homicide of A + homicide of B), if from a single act"],["<b>Praeter intentionem</b>","The result is greater than intended","Liable for the result, mitigated under Art. 13(3) (no intention to commit so grave a wrong), unless the means used show intent (e.g., a deadly weapon)"]]},
{k:"h",x:"Doctrines"},
{k:"cases",ids:["iligan","mananquil","brobst","garcia"]},
{k:"gate",id:"g-4a"},
{k:"box",tone:"yellow",title:"Traps",x:["Art. 4(1) needs an <b>intentional felony</b> as the starting point. A <b>lawful</b> act (e.g., a lawful arrest) that accidentally causes injury → Art. 12(4) accident, not Art. 4(1).","The victim's <b>refusal of treatment</b> or a <b>pre-existing illness</b> does not break the chain (San Beda MCQs 9 and 11).","<b>Praeter intentionem</b> is not available where the weapon or the manner of attack shows intent to cause the result (e.g., a knife to the chest).","An <b>impossible crime</b> cannot combine with Art. 4(1): if any felony results (e.g., physical injuries), the offender is liable for that felony instead."]},
{k:"exam",x:"“Under Art. 4(1), one who commits a felony is liable for all its direct, natural and logical consequences, even if unintended. The felonious act need not be the immediate cause; it is enough that it was the proximate cause of the result, unbroken by an efficient intervening cause (People v. Iligan). The victim's pre-existing illness is not such a cause (Garcia v. People).”"},
{k:"drill",x:[["Garcia, but the victim died a week later of a heart attack while jogging.","The chain may be broken: an independent cause and too remote → physical injuries only."],["Iligan, but the victim walked home safely and was hit by a car the next morning on his way to work.","An efficient intervening cause → no liability for the death."],["A punches B, B falls, hits his head on the pavement and dies.","Homicide (Art. 4(1)) with praeter intentionem as mitigating (Art. 13(3))."]]}
]},

{k:"a4b",n:"Art. 4(2) · Impossible crime",s:"Inherent impossibility; inadequate or ineffectual means",a:["4"],b:[
{k:"codal",a:"4"},
{k:"h",x:"Reyes: paragraph 2"},
{k:"ul",x:["<b>Rationale</b>: positivist. The offender is punished for his <b>criminal tendency or propensity</b>, because objectively no crime was committed, but subjectively he is a criminal.","<b>Requisites</b>: (1) the act would be an offense against <b>persons or property</b>; (2) done with <b>evil intent</b>; (3) its accomplishment is <b>inherently impossible</b>, or the means employed are <b>inadequate or ineffectual</b>; (4) the act does <b>not constitute a violation of another provision</b> of the RPC.","“<b>Offense against persons or property</b>” covers the felonies in Title Eight (crimes against persons: parricide, murder, homicide, physical injuries, rape) and Title Ten (crimes against property: robbery, theft, estafa…). Kidnapping (liberty), treason, falsification and the like are <b>not</b> included.","<b>Inherent impossibility</b>: <b>legal</b> (the intended acts, even if completed, would not be a crime, e.g., stealing one's own property, killing a corpse) or <b>physical/factual</b> (an extraneous circumstance unknown to the actor prevents it: an empty pocket, an absent victim (<i>Intod</i>), a worthless check (<i>Jacinto</i>)).","<b>Inadequate means</b>: too little poison. <b>Ineffectual means</b>: sugar instead of arsenic, an unloaded gun. If the means were <b>adequate</b> but the result did not happen → frustrated felony, not an impossible crime.","<b>Penalty</b>: Art. 59 (arresto mayor or a fine), considering the social danger and degree of criminality shown.","There is <b>no attempted or frustrated impossible crime</b>: the offender has already performed all the acts."]},
{k:"tbl",head:["","Impossible crime","Attempted / frustrated"],rows:[["Why the felony was not produced","The crime could never be accomplished (inherent impossibility, inadequate/ineffectual means)","It could have been accomplished, but an external cause intervened"],["Example","Shooting into an empty room (Intod)","A forged ticket noticed by an alert clerk (Balmores)"]]},
{k:"h",x:"Doctrines"},
{k:"cases",ids:["balmores","intod","domasian","jacinto"]},
{k:"gate",id:"g-imp"},
{k:"box",tone:"yellow",title:"Traps",x:["<b>Kidnapping is never an impossible crime</b> (a crime against liberty, and consummated once liberty is taken) (<i>Domasian</i>; 2014 Bar).","If the act results in <b>another felony</b> (e.g., the 'poison' only causes an upset stomach), the offender is liable for that felony (physical injuries), not an impossible crime (2014 Bar).","<b>Discovery by an alert victim</b> is an external cause: an attempt, not impossibility (<i>Balmores</i>).","In <i>Intod</i>, the shooting also damaged the house, yet the Court still convicted for an impossible crime. If asked, note that malicious mischief or damage to property could arguably be a 'violation of another provision', but follow <i>Intod</i>."]},
{k:"exam",x:"“An impossible crime is committed when the act would be an offense against persons or property, done with evil intent, but its accomplishment is inherently impossible or the means are inadequate or ineffectual, and the act constitutes no other felony (Art. 4(2)). Firing at a room where the intended victim is absent is factually impossible murder (Intod v. CA).”"},
{k:"drill",x:[["Intod, but Bernardina's son-in-law was in the room and was hit.","No longer impossible: liable for the crime against the son-in-law (error in personae / Art. 4(1))."],["Jacinto, but the check was good and was encashed.","Consummated qualified theft."],["A pours 'poison' that turns out to be sugar into B's coffee.","Impossible crime (ineffectual means)."],["A pours too little arsenic; B survives only because of a strong constitution.","Inadequate means → impossible crime; if the dose was adequate and B was saved by treatment → frustrated murder."]]}
]},

{k:"a5",n:"Art. 5 · Duty of the court",s:"Acts not punishable; excessive penalties",a:["5"],b:[
{k:"codal",a:"5"},
{k:"ul",x:["<b>Par. 1</b> rests on <b><i>nullum crimen, nulla poena sine lege</i></b>: the court must <b>acquit</b> if the act is not punishable, even if it deserves repression, then report to the Chief Executive through the DOJ recommending legislation.","<b>Par. 2</b>: the court must still <b>impose the penalty</b> (judges cannot refuse to apply a harsh law; <i>dura lex sed lex</i>) and <b>may not suspend</b> execution, but it may recommend <b>executive clemency</b> where the penalty is clearly excessive given the degree of malice and the injury caused.","Par. 2 does not apply to special laws (it speaks of “provisions of this Code”). It is the basis for courts recommending pardon or commutation."]},
{k:"box",tone:"yellow",title:"Traps",x:["The court does <b>not</b> lower the penalty itself; it imposes the penalty and recommends clemency.","The court does <b>not</b> convict for an act not punishable by law, however reprehensible."]},
{k:"exam",x:"“Under Art. 5, the court cannot convict for an act not punished by law (nullum crimen, nulla poena sine lege); it must acquit and report to the Chief Executive, through the DOJ, recommending legislation. When the penalty is clearly excessive, it must still impose it, without suspending execution, and may recommend executive clemency.”"}
]},

{k:"a6",n:"Art. 6 · Stages",s:"Attempted, frustrated, consummated",a:["6"],b:[
{k:"codal",a:"6"},
{k:"h",x:"Reyes: development of a crime"},
{k:"ul",x:["<b>Internal acts</b> (ideas) → not punishable. <b>External acts</b>: <b>preparatory</b> acts (buying poison, carrying a weapon, conspiracy and proposal) → ordinarily not punishable unless the law makes them independent crimes; <b>acts of execution</b> → punishable as attempted, frustrated or consummated.","<b>Attempt</b>: the offender <b>commences</b> the commission directly by <b>overt acts</b> (external acts with a direct connection to a specific crime that, if continued, would logically ripen into it) and does not perform all the acts of execution, because of a cause or accident <b>other than his own spontaneous desistance</b>.","<b>Indeterminate offense</b> (<i>Lamahang</i>): if the acts do not show which crime was intended, the offender is liable only for what they clearly show; intent is judged from the nature of the acts, not from his later admissions.","<b>Spontaneous desistance</b> is an <b>absolutory cause</b> in the attempted stage only. But the acts already done may themselves be a crime (e.g., trespass, physical injuries, illegal possession).","<b>Subjective phase</b>: the portion of the acts from the beginning up to the point where the offender still has control over his acts. If interrupted here (not by desistance) → <b>attempted</b>. <b>Objective phase</b>: once all acts are done and only the result is missing → <b>frustrated</b> if the result does not follow for causes independent of his will.","<b>Frustrated homicide or murder</b> requires that the offender performed all the acts of execution, which usually means a <b>mortal wound</b> that would have caused death but for timely medical help. A non-mortal wound with intent to kill → <b>attempted</b>. No intent to kill → physical injuries (<i>Ravelo</i>).","<b>Intent to kill</b> is shown by the weapon used, the nature, location and number of wounds, the manner of attack, and words before, during or after (<i>Kalalo</i>: four shots).","<b>Consummated</b>: all the elements necessary for its execution and accomplishment are present."]},
{k:"tbl",head:["Factor","Point"],rows:[["Nature of the offense","Arson: consummated once any part of the building is burned; some crimes are consummated in a single act"],["Elements of the felony","Theft: consummated by taking (Valenzuela); estafa: by damage; rape: by any penetration (Orita)"],["Manner of committing","Formal crimes (slander, false testimony): consummated in one act, no attempt. Crimes consummated by mere attempt or proposal (flight to an enemy country, corruption of minors). Crimes by omission: no attempt. Crimes needing two persons (betting, bribery): consummated by agreement; a rejected offer is an attempt"]]},
{k:"h",x:"Doctrines"},
{k:"cases",ids:["eduave","orita","campuhan","agao","adiao","valenzuela","lamahang","salvilla","borinaga","sypio","ravelo","kalalo"]},
{k:"gate",id:"g-stage"},
{k:"box",tone:"yellow",title:"Traps",x:["<b>No frustrated theft</b> (Valenzuela) and <b>no frustrated rape</b> (Orita). Any problem saying “frustrated theft” is testing this.","<b>Desistance after a mortal wound</b> is not desistance: the stage is already frustrated (2022 Bar: Sisa threw down the knife after the stab wounds → frustrated parricide if the wounds were mortal).","<b>Attempted arson</b>: pouring gasoline and lighting a match, caught before any fire → attempted (2019 Bar).","<b>Proposal is not an overt act</b>: asking someone to kidnap a girl is not attempted kidnapping (1996 Bar).","<b>Physical injuries</b> have no attempted or frustrated stage; they are formal crimes in effect (2017 Bar)."]},
{k:"exam",x:"“A felony is frustrated when the offender performs all the acts of execution but the felony is not produced for reasons independent of his will; it is attempted when he commences by overt acts but does not perform all of them. In homicide, frustration requires that the subjective phase be complete, as when the offender inflicts a mortal wound believing the victim dead (U.S. v. Eduave); if he knows the victim escaped unhurt in a vital part, it is only attempted (People v. Sy Pio).”"},
{k:"drill",x:[["Sy Pio, but Tan fell and lay still, and Sy Pio walked off thinking him dead.","Subjective phase complete → frustrated murder (like Eduave), if the wound was mortal."],["Eduave, but he stopped after a shallow cut and walked away of his own accord.","Spontaneous desistance → not liable for attempted murder, but liable for the physical injuries inflicted."],["Valenzuela, but caught inside the store with the Tide still on the pushcart.","Still consummated theft: the taking (apoderamiento) is complete once he gains possession with intent to gain; the chance to dispose of the goods is immaterial."],["Lamahang, but he says in his statement he wanted to rob the store.","Still judged by the nature of the acts; the admission alone does not make the robbery attempt determinate."],["Campuhan, but the medical exam shows a fresh laceration of the labia.","Consummated (statutory) rape."]]}
]},

{k:"a7",n:"Art. 7 · Light felonies",s:"When punishable",a:["7"],b:[
{k:"codal",a:"7"},
{k:"ul",x:["<b>Light felonies</b>: those punished with <b>arresto menor or a fine not exceeding ₱40,000</b>, or both (Art. 9, as amended by RA 10951).","<b>Rule</b>: punishable only when <b>consummated</b>. The injury is so slight that attempts are not worth punishing (Albert). <b>Exception</b>: light felonies against <b>persons or property</b> (e.g., slight physical injuries, Art. 266; theft under Art. 309(7)–(8); malicious mischief) are punishable even if attempted or frustrated, because they show moral depravity.","Examples of light felonies: slight physical injuries; theft of items of small value or prompted by hunger; alteration of boundary marks; malicious mischief of small value; intriguing against honor; alarms and scandals; light threats; light coercion; slander by deed (not serious).","<b>Only principals and accomplices</b> are liable for light felonies; accessories are not (Art. 16)."]},
{k:"exam",x:"“Under Art. 7, light felonies are punishable only when consummated, except those against persons or property.”"}
]},

{k:"a8",n:"Art. 8 · Conspiracy & proposal",s:"As a crime vs. as a mode",a:["8"],b:[
{k:"codal",a:"8"},
{k:"ul",x:["<b>General rule</b>: conspiracy and proposal are <b>preparatory acts</b> and are not punishable. <b>Exception</b>: only where the law specially provides a penalty: conspiracy to commit <b>treason</b> (Art. 115), <b>coup d'état, rebellion, insurrection</b> (Art. 136), <b>sedition</b> (Art. 141), and <b>monopolies</b> (Art. 186); proposal to commit <b>treason</b> (Art. 115) and <b>coup d'état, rebellion, insurrection</b> (Art. 136). (Some special laws punish conspiracy too, e.g., under RA 9165 § 26.)","There is <b>no common-law crime of conspiracy</b> in the Philippines (<i>U.S. v. Lim Buanco</i>).","<b>Requisites of conspiracy</b>: two or more persons; an agreement concerning a felony; a decision to commit it. <b>Proposal</b>: a person who has decided to commit a felony proposes its execution to another. A proposal that is <b>accepted</b> becomes a conspiracy. The proposal need not be accepted to be punished (where punishable).","<b>Conspiracy as a mode of liability</b>: once proven, the <b>act of one is the act of all</b>, regardless of each conspirator's degree of participation. It must be proven as clearly as the crime itself; it may be <b>implied</b> from acts showing a common purpose and design. <b>Mere presence</b> or knowledge, without overt participation, is not conspiracy (<i>Iligan</i>: Asis acquitted).","If the conspirators go on to <b>commit</b> the crime (e.g., actual rebellion), the conspiracy is absorbed; they are liable for the crime itself.","A conspirator who <b>desists before</b> any overt act is not liable for the crime (except for the conspiracy itself where it is punishable)."]},
{k:"box",tone:"yellow",title:"Traps",x:["<b>Conspiracy to commit coup d'état abroad</b> (Hong Kong): punishable under Art. 136, but is it triable here? Coup d'état is a crime against public order (Title Three), not in Art. 2(5); the conspiracy was formed outside our territory. Argue territoriality first (San Beda Q5).","Conspiracy to commit <b>murder</b> or <b>robbery</b> is not a crime in itself (there is no penalty for it); the parties are liable only once they commit overt acts.","A proposal to commit <b>sedition</b> is not punished as such under the RPC (only inciting to sedition, Art. 142)."]},
{k:"exam",x:"“Under Art. 8, conspiracy and proposal are punishable only where the law specially provides a penalty, as in treason, rebellion, coup d'état and sedition. As a mode of committing a crime, conspiracy makes the act of one the act of all, but it must be proven by clear evidence of a common design; mere presence is not enough.”"}
]},

{k:"a9",n:"Art. 9 · Classification",s:"Grave, less grave, light",a:["9"],b:[
{k:"codal",a:"9"},
{k:"ul",x:["Gravity depends on the <b>penalty</b> attached by law (by reference to Art. 25).","<b>Grave</b>: capital punishment or a penalty that in <b>any</b> of its periods is afflictive. If the penalty has two or more distinct penalties, the <b>higher</b> must be afflictive (prisión correccional to prisión mayor → grave). <b>Less grave</b>: a penalty that in its <b>maximum</b> period is correctional. <b>Light</b>: arresto menor or a fine not exceeding ₱40,000.","A fine of <b>exactly ₱40,000</b> → light felony under Art. 9, even though Art. 26 calls ₱40,000 a correctional penalty: <b>Art. 9 prevails</b> for classifying felonies, Art. 26 for classifying fines (e.g., for prescription).","Why it matters: Art. 48 (complex crimes involve grave or less grave felonies), Art. 7 (light felonies), Art. 16 (no accessories in light felonies), prescription (Art. 90), and duration of preventive detention."]},
{k:"tbl",head:["Class (Art. 25)","Penalties"],rows:[["Capital","Death (its imposition is prohibited by RA 9346)"],["Afflictive","Reclusion perpetua; reclusion temporal; perpetual or temporary absolute disqualification; perpetual or temporary special disqualification; prisión mayor"],["Correctional","Prisión correccional; arresto mayor; suspension; destierro"],["Light","Arresto menor; public censure"],["Fines (Art. 26, RA 10951)","Afflictive > ₱1,200,000; correctional ₱40,000–₱1,200,000; light < ₱40,000"]]},
{k:"exam",x:"“Under Art. 9, grave felonies carry the capital penalty or penalties afflictive in any of their periods; less grave felonies, penalties correctional in their maximum period; light felonies, arresto menor or a fine not exceeding ₱40,000.”"}
]},

{k:"a10",n:"Art. 10 · Special laws",s:"Supplementary application",a:["10"],b:[
{k:"codal",a:"10"},
{k:"ul",x:["A <b>special law</b> is a penal law that punishes acts not defined and penalized by the RPC (<i>U.S. v. Serapio</i>).","<b>First clause</b>: offenses under special laws are governed by those laws (special law prevails over general law). <b>Second clause</b>, “the soul of the article”: the RPC is <b>supplementary</b> unless the special law provides otherwise. So the RPC's provisions on conspiracy, subsidiary imprisonment, civil liability, etc. may apply to special laws (<i>Ladonga v. People</i>: Art. 8 conspiracy applied to BP 22).","Where a special law <b>adopts the RPC nomenclature of penalties</b> (e.g., RA 7659's amendments; RA 9165), the RPC rules on graduation of penalties and modifying circumstances apply (<i>People v. Simon</i>).","Crimes under special laws are generally <b>mala prohibita</b>: intent and good faith are immaterial; there are generally no attempted or frustrated stages unless the law provides for them."]},
{k:"box",tone:"yellow",title:"Traps",x:["<b>Illegal possession of a firearm</b> (San Beda Q2): malum prohibitum. Good faith (the agency issued it) is not a defense to the crime itself, but <b>animus possidendi</b> (intent to possess) is still required for possession offenses, and a security guard who holds a firearm issued by his agency for duty may lack the intent to possess it unlawfully. Argue both sides.","<b>Plunder</b> is a special law but malum <b>in se</b> (<i>Estrada v. Sandiganbayan</i>): the label does not always decide the question."]},
{k:"exam",x:"“Under Art. 10, offenses under special laws are governed by those laws, but the RPC applies suppletorily unless the special law provides otherwise (Ladonga v. People).”"}
]},

{k:"a11",n:"Art. 11 · Justifying",s:"Self-defense, relatives, strangers, necessity, duty, obedience",a:["11"],b:[
{k:"codal",a:"11"},
{k:"h",x:"Reyes: the idea"},
{k:"ul",x:["The act is <b>in accordance with law</b>, so the actor is deemed not to have transgressed it: <b>no crime and no criminal</b>, hence no criminal and no civil liability, <b>except ¶4</b> (the persons benefited pay in proportion to the benefit, Art. 101).","<b>Burden</b>: the accused who invokes it admits the act, so he must prove it by <b>clear and convincing evidence</b>, relying on the strength of his own evidence, not the weakness of the prosecution's.","<b>Imputability</b>: the act may be ascribed to the person as its author. <b>Responsibility</b>: the obligation to suffer its consequences. <b>Guilt</b> is an element of responsibility."]},
{k:"h",x:"¶1 Self-defense"},
{k:"ul",x:["<b>Unlawful aggression</b> is the <i>sine qua non</i>: without it there is no self-defense, complete or incomplete. It must be an <b>actual physical assault</b> or a threat that is <b>offensive and positively strong</b>, with danger that is actual or imminent. Not aggression: insulting words; a mere threatening attitude; a hand in the pocket. Aggression: a slap (an attack on dignity), aiming a gun, rushing with a bolo.","It must <b>still exist</b>: when the aggressor flees or is disarmed, it ends (<i>Alconga</i>); a retreat to get a better position does not end it. <b>Retaliation</b> is not self-defense.","<b>Agreement to fight</b>: there is no unlawful aggression between parties to a fight they agreed on, unless one attacks before the agreed time and place.","<b>Reasonable necessity of the means</b>: <b>rational equivalence, not material equality</b>. Consider the weapons, size, strength, number of attackers, place, and occasion (<i>Mack</i>; <i>Sumicad</i>). The defender is not expected to deliberate coolly or aim at less vital parts. No duty to retreat (stand your ground). The number and location of wounds can belie the plea.","<b>Lack of sufficient provocation</b> is present when there was no provocation, when it was not sufficient, when it was not given by the defender, or when it was not proximate and immediate to the aggression. <b>Sufficient</b> = proportionate to the aggression and adequate to stir it.","<b>Rights</b> defended include life, limb, <b>honor</b> (<i>Luague</i>, <i>De la Cruz</i>; excessive in <i>Jaurigue</i>), <b>property</b> (old rule: the property defense requires an attack on the person, <i>Apolinar</i>; now Civil Code Art. 429 allows reasonable force against an actual or threatened unlawful invasion, <i>Narvaez</i>), and <b>reputation</b> (an adequate reply, <i>Chua Hiong</i>, but not an unrelated attack, <i>Pelayo</i>)."]},
{k:"box",tone:"green",title:"Battered Woman Syndrome",x:["<i>Genosa</i> (2004): recognized BWS but required proof of the <b>cycle of violence at least twice</b>: tension-building → acute battering → tranquil, loving phase. Expert testimony is needed.","<b>RA 9262 § 26</b>: a woman found by the court to suffer from BWS incurs <b>no criminal and no civil liability</b>, even if the elements of self-defense are absent."]},
{k:"h",x:"¶2 Relatives · ¶3 Strangers"},
{k:"ul",x:["Relatives: spouse; ascendants; descendants; legitimate, natural or adopted siblings; affines in the same degrees; consanguinity within the <b>fourth</b> civil degree (first cousins). A wife's first cousin is <b>not</b> covered (affinity in the 4th degree) → defense of a stranger (2016 Bar).","¶2's third requisite: if the relative gave provocation, the defender <b>took no part</b> in it. The relative's own provocation does not bar the defender.","¶3: any person not in ¶2 is a <b>stranger</b>, even a close friend. Third requisite: <b>not induced by revenge, resentment or other evil motive</b>. A pre-existing grudge does not bar the defense if the actual motive was to save the stranger (“be not <i>induced</i>”).","Honest belief: as in <i>Esmedia</i>, the aggression is judged as it reasonably appeared to the defender."]},
{k:"h",x:"¶4 Avoidance of greater evil"},
{k:"ul",x:["Requisites: the evil actually exists (not merely expected); the injury feared is <b>greater</b> than the injury done; there is <b>no other practical and less harmful means</b>.","The evil must not be brought about by the actor's own <b>negligence or violation of law</b> (<i>Ty</i>).","“Damage to another” covers injury to persons, property and <b>honor</b> (<i>Norma Hernandez</i>). A life for a life is not a greater evil (<i>Dudley</i>). Abortion to save the mother's life is the classic example.","The <b>only</b> justifying circumstance with civil liability: the persons benefited pay, in proportion to the benefit."]},
{k:"h",x:"¶5 Duty, right or office · ¶6 Obedience"},
{k:"ul",x:["¶5 requisites: acted in the performance of a duty or in the lawful exercise of a right or office; the injury is the <b>necessary consequence</b> of the due performance of the duty. No unlawful aggression is needed (<i>Delima</i>). Only the first requisite present → privileged mitigating (Art. 69) (<i>Oanis</i>, <i>Belbes</i>).","Officers may use force only as is reasonably necessary to arrest, overcome resistance or prevent escape; never on a sleeping or surrendering suspect.","¶6 requisites: an order by a superior; for a lawful purpose; the means used by the subordinate are lawful. An illegal order does not justify; but a subordinate who obeys in <b>good faith</b>, unaware of the illegality and without negligence, lacks criminal intent (<i>Beronilla</i>)."]},
{k:"box",tone:"blue",title:"Incomplete justification",x:["Unlawful aggression + the majority of the requisites → <b>privileged mitigating</b>: 1 or 2 degrees lower (Art. 69).","Unlawful aggression with fewer requisites → <b>ordinary mitigating</b> (Art. 13(1)).","No unlawful aggression → <b>nothing</b> under self-defense; look to other mitigating circumstances (provocation, passion)."]},
{k:"h",x:"Doctrines"},
{k:"cases",ids:["boholst","alconga","mack","sumicad","genosa","luague","delacruz","jaurigue","apolinar","bumanglag","narvaez","chuahiong","pelayo","esmedia","normahernandez","dudley","ty","delima","belbes","beronilla"]},
{k:"gate",id:"g-sd"},
{k:"box",tone:"yellow",title:"Traps",x:["<b>Aggressor fleeing</b> → aggression has ceased → no self-defense (<i>Alconga</i>; 2002 Bar: shooting a rapist who had run away).","<b>The thief caught in the act</b> who wrests the owner's gun and kills her: the owner was lawfully defending her property, so <b>the thief</b> was the unlawful aggressor (2019 Bar, Mr. R and Mrs. V).","<b>Defense of a stranger with an evil motive</b>: the third requisite fails only if the motive actually induced the act (San Beda Q1: Ric's revenge thought; and Morty turned out to be his brother, so the defense is of a relative after all; ¶2 has no motive requirement).","<b>Euthanasia</b> is not justified; sacrificing the fetus to save the mother is (¶4) (1990 Bar).","<b>Fulfillment of duty</b> is not self-defense: Delima was justified even though the aggression had ceased."]},
{k:"exam",x:"“Self-defense requires (1) unlawful aggression, (2) reasonable necessity of the means employed to prevent or repel it, and (3) lack of sufficient provocation by the defender (Art. 11(1)). Unlawful aggression is indispensable; it must be actual or imminent and must still exist when the defense is made. Once the aggressor flees, it ceases, and what follows is retaliation (People v. Alconga).”"},
{k:"drill",x:[["Alconga, but the fleeing aggressor runs to his jeep for a gun.","Aggression continues (he retreats for a better position) → pursuit is allowed."],["Boholst-Caballero, but the husband had let go and walked away.","Aggression ceased → stabbing is retaliation."],["Jaurigue, but on a dark, deserted path.","Like De la Cruz: complete defense of honor is likely."],["Apolinar, but the thief swings a bolo at the owner.","Unlawful aggression on the person → the shooting can be justified."],["Delima, but Napilon had raised his hands to surrender.","Not a necessary consequence of duty → homicide (cf. Oanis)."],["Beronilla, but he had received the Volckmann radiogram.","Good faith is gone → murder."],["Dudley in the Philippines.","¶4 fails (a life for a life is not a greater evil)."]]}
]}
];

// Decision gates: click-through trees. Each node: q, y (yes → node id or end), n.
window.GATES = {
"g-4a":{n:"Is the offender liable for the unintended result? (Art. 4(1))",start:"a",nodes:{
a:{q:"Was the initial act an <b>intentional felony</b>?",y:"b",n:{end:"Not Art. 4(1). If the act was lawful and the injury accidental → Art. 12(4); if negligent → Art. 365 (culpa).",tone:"r"}},
b:{q:"Was the result the <b>direct, natural and logical consequence</b> of that felony (no efficient intervening cause)?",y:"c",n:{end:"No liability for the result; liable only for the felony actually committed (e.g., physical injuries). Example: Urbano (tetanus days later).",tone:"r"}},
c:{q:"Was the result <b>greater than intended</b>?",y:{end:"Liable for the result, with praeter intentionem as mitigating (Art. 13(3)) unless the means show intent (Garcia v. People).",tone:"y"},n:"d"},
d:{q:"Did the blow land on a <b>different person</b> who was also present?",y:{end:"Aberratio ictus → usually a complex crime (Art. 48).",tone:"g"},n:{end:"Error in personae → liable for the crime committed; Art. 49 penalty rule if the crimes differ.",tone:"g"}}}},
"g-imp":{n:"Is it an impossible crime? (Art. 4(2))",start:"a",nodes:{
a:{q:"Would the act be an offense against <b>persons or property</b>?",y:"b",n:{end:"No impossible crime (e.g., kidnapping, falsification). Check if another crime was consummated (Domasian).",tone:"r"}},
b:{q:"Was it done with <b>evil intent</b>?",y:"c",n:{end:"No impossible crime: no criminal propensity.",tone:"r"}},
c:{q:"Did it fail because it was <b>inherently impossible</b> or the means were <b>inadequate/ineffectual</b> (not because someone intervened)?",y:"d",n:{end:"Attempted or frustrated felony, e.g., the alert clerk in Balmores.",tone:"y"}},
d:{q:"Does the act <b>constitute another felony</b> (e.g., physical injuries)?",y:{end:"Liable for that other felony instead.",tone:"y"},n:{end:"IMPOSSIBLE CRIME (Art. 4(2); penalty under Art. 59). Intod; Jacinto.",tone:"g"}}}},
"g-stage":{n:"Which stage? (Art. 6)",start:"a",nodes:{
a:{q:"Were there <b>overt acts</b> directly connected to a <b>specific</b> crime?",y:"b",n:{end:"Only preparatory or indeterminate acts → no attempt of that crime; liable only for what the acts clearly show (Lamahang: attempted trespass).",tone:"r"}},
b:{q:"Are <b>all the elements</b> of the crime present (e.g., any penetration; taking; death)?",y:{end:"CONSUMMATED (Orita; Valenzuela; Salvilla).",tone:"g"},n:"c"},
c:{q:"Did the offender perform <b>all the acts of execution</b> (subjective phase complete; for homicide, usually a mortal wound and the belief that it is done)?",y:{end:"FRUSTRATED, if the result failed for causes independent of his will (Eduave). Not possible for rape or theft.",tone:"y"},n:"d"},
d:{q:"Did he stop by his own <b>spontaneous desistance</b>?",y:{end:"Not liable for the attempt; liable only for the acts already committed, if they are a crime.",tone:"b"},n:{end:"ATTEMPTED (Sy Pio; Kalalo: shots that missed; Campuhan).",tone:"y"}}}},
"g-sd":{n:"Justified as self-defense? (Art. 11(1))",start:"a",nodes:{
a:{q:"Was there <b>unlawful aggression</b>: an actual or imminent, real attack, still existing at the moment of defense?",y:"b",n:{end:"No self-defense, complete or incomplete. Retaliation (Alconga, second stage). Look to Art. 13 for other mitigating circumstances. (BWS under RA 9262 § 26 is the exception.)",tone:"r"}},
b:{q:"Were the <b>means reasonably necessary</b> (rational equivalence: weapons, size, place, occasion)?",y:"c",n:{end:"Incomplete self-defense → privileged mitigating, Art. 69 (Jaurigue; Narvaez).",tone:"y"}},
c:{q:"Was there <b>lack of sufficient provocation</b> by the defender?",y:{end:"COMPLETE SELF-DEFENSE → acquittal, no civil liability (Mack; Boholst-Caballero).",tone:"g"},n:{end:"Incomplete self-defense → privileged mitigating, Art. 69.",tone:"y"}}}}
};
